⏺ All checks pass. Here's a summary of what was wrong and what I changed in
  rhombicIcosahedron.html:

  What was wrong

  The old file had 24 vertices that weren't the actual vertices of a rhombic 
  icosahedron (it mixed up coordinate orders like [a, b, 0] vs [b, a, 0]), and a
  hand-written edge list full of duplicated and spurious adjacencies. The real
  rhombic icosahedron has 22 vertices, 40 edges, 20 rhombic faces (degrees 10[3]
  + 10[4] + 2[5], D<sub>5d</sub> symmetry).

  The fix

  - Correct geometry — I pulled the exact vertex coordinates from D. McCooey's
    Visual Polyhedra (https://dmccooey.com/polyhedra/RhombicIcosahedron.html)
    (golden-ratio constants C0–C8, unit edge length), with all 20 rhombic faces.
  - Edges derived from faces — instead of hand-listing adjacencies (the source
    of the bug), the 40 unique edges are computed from the face loop, so they
    can never be inconsistent. I verified in node: every face is a perfect
    unit-edge rhombus (deviation ~1e-16), every edge appears in exactly 2 faces,
    and degrees match dmccooey's data exactly.
  - Proper zonohedron rendering — faces are now drawn as shaded filled rhombi
    with painter's-algorithm depth sorting and Lambert lighting (antique-gold
    palette matching your site's math-art.css), with the edge skeleton on top.
  - Random 3D tumbling — the object rotates about an axis whose direction
    random-walks: a new random spin direction/speed is picked every 4–9 seconds
    and eased into smoothly, so it tumbles unpredictably through all
    orientations.
  - Extras — drag to spin manually (pointer events, works on touch), spin-speed
    slider, wireframe toggle, vertex-index toggle, hi-dpi crisp canvas, and
    attribution to the data source. The page now uses your site's
    cloth-green/paper design tokens.

  Verification: JS syntax passes node --check; a 20,000-frame simulation of the
  tumble confirms the rotation matrix stays orthonormal and all projections stay
  finite.

